response:
To improve efficiency, you can consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This allows you to work with very large files without running out of memory. Here's how you can modify the code above to use memory-mapped files:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(fileName1, fileName2)
    % Read text files as memory-mapped files
    mmFile1 = mmap('r', fileName1);
    mmFile2 = mmap('r', fileName2);

    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(mmFile1(:)));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(mmFile2(:)));

    % Find unique words
    uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

    % Close memory-mapped files
    close(mmFile1);
    close(mmFile2);
end
```

In this function, `findUniqueWords` takes two file names as input and returns the unique words found in the first file that are not present in the second file. The `mmmap` function is used to open the text files as memory-mapped files, which allows us to read them in chunks instead of loading the entire contents into memory. This makes it possible to handle very large files without running out of memory.

Note that memory-mapped files can be slower than reading the entire file into memory due to the additional overhead of accessing the disk. However, for extremely large files, this approach can significantly reduce the memory footprint of your program and make it more efficient overall.

