To implement bubble sort in C++ with minimal memory usage and error prevention, we'll use an iterative approach instead of recursion. This will allow us to avoid stack overflow issues and minimize the number of function calls required, which can lead to improved performance and lower memory usage.

Here's an implementation of bubble sort in C++ using an iterative approach:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true; // flag for checking if any swaps were made
    int temp; // temporary variable for swapping elements

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1 && swapped; i++) {
        swapped = false; // reset swapped flag at beginning of each iteration

        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) { // compare adjacent elements
                temp = arr[j]; // swap elements if necessary
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
                swapped = true; // set swapped flag if swap was made
            }
        }
    }
}
```

